__pycache__/
*.pyc
.venv/
.venv-win/
*.egg-info/
build/
dist/
zxspectrum/_native/*.so
zxspectrum/_native/*.pyd
zxspectrum/_native/*.o
zxspectrum/_native/_z80.c
roms/*
!roms/.gitkeep
.pytest_cache/

# Fetched reference copy of z80_gen.py (github.com/floooh/chips) -- a
# reading aid for scripts/generate_z80_dispatch.py, which ports its
# algorithm to C++; not committed since it's someone else's source tree,
# not this project's, and generate_z80_dispatch.py is the actual artifact.
.chips-codegen-src/

# Fetched ZEXALL/ZEXDOC (GPL v2.0, github.com/agn453/ZEXALL) -- a standalone
# Z80 exerciser used only as a test fixture, not linked into the emulator;
# fetched rather than committed to avoid vendoring a second project's tree.
.zexall-src/

# Fetched/generated ROM disassembly (copyrighted -- Amstrad / Logan &
# O'Hara / Dymond -- same treatment as roms/: never committed, see README)
.rom-disassembly-src/
rom_disassembly/

# Left over from before the game disassemblies moved to their own repository
# (github.com/jonsole/zx-spectrum-disassemblies, checked out here as the
# game-disassemblies submodule). Kept ignored so an existing clone's output
# does not suddenly show up as untracked; safe to delete.
.manicminer-disassembly-src/
.fairlight-disassembly-src/
game_disassembly/

# Game snapshots and tape images (copyrighted -- same treatment as roms/ and
# the disassemblies above). The .sna files that ARE committed here are the Z80
# exercisers, which are freely distributable, and tapes/ holds only what
# scripts/make_test_tape.py generates -- so both are listed back in by name
# rather than the whole directory being left unguarded.
snapshots/*.sna
!snapshots/z80full.sna
!snapshots/zexall.sna
!snapshots/zexccfb.sna
!snapshots/zexdoc.sna
# .z80 has no exceptions: save_snapshot writes .sna or .z80 by the path's
# extension, and every .z80 that lands here is a game paused mid-run.
snapshots/*.z80
tapes/*.tap
tapes/*.tzx
tapes/*.wav
tapes/*.csw
!tapes/loading-test.tap
!tapes/loading-test.tzx

# Fetched sjasmplus binary (github.com/z00m128/sjasmplus) -- a build tool,
# not this project's own code; fetched by the LDIR contention example's
# build task rather than committed. Scoped to its own directory rather than
# all of tools/, which also holds committed things (trace_viewer.html).
tools/sjasmplus/

# Scratch git worktrees created for isolated agent runs -- each is a full
# checkout of the repo, not project content. The tracked parts of .claude/
# (agents/, skills/) are committed normally.
.claude/worktrees/
trace_screen.rgb

# Captured bus traces (zx_server --trace-log / the start_trace MCP tool), and
# the copies the trace-log tests write beside themselves. Outputs, not source
# -- the one committed trace is tests/data/visualz80_reference.txt, which is
# the reference the tests diff against.
*.zxtrace
zx_test_*.txt
# ...and the SLD fixture symbol_tests writes beside them.
zx_test_*.sld

# Scope screenshots and waveform CSVs pulled off the Rigol MSO5074
# (tools/rigol/) -- bench captures, not project source.
captures/

# examples/filmation: every game folder has the same shape (scripts/layout.py
# says it), and three of its folders are never carried:
#
#   output/      what build.py makes on every launch: the snapshot, its SLD,
#                the listing
#   generated/   the .s build.py writes from the carried JSON and art: the
#                sprites, the graphic table, the rooms, the font, the charms
#   extracted/   what an extractor (kl_extract.py, pg_extract.py) pulls out of
#                your own copy of the original: the packed sprites, rooms,
#                font, quest and sound, and the font sheet unpacked from them
#
# The flow this repository follows:
#
#   your game --(extract, ONCE)--> extracted/ --> metadata/*.json, art/*.png
#                                                     |
#                                                     v  (build.py, every time)
#                                                generated/*.s --> output/
#
# metadata/ and art/ are CARRIED and are what you edit, by hand or in the
# designers. Nothing regenerates them -- that is the whole point, and it is why
# build.py stops rather than re-running an extractor. What is in extracted/ is
# Ultimate's, in the games' own packed shapes: it is yours alone, and once the
# JSON exists nothing needs it again except remaking a sheet. The pixel nudges
# in graphics.json cannot be re-made from any of it -- adj.py harvests them from
# a RUNNING game -- which is why that file is carried and every tool that
# writes it merges rather than replacing.
#
# examples/filmation/output/ is the engine's unit tests' assembled programs,
# from engine/tests/run_tests.py.
examples/filmation/*/output/
examples/filmation/*/generated/
examples/filmation/*/extracted/
examples/filmation/output/

# isoblocks: everything painter/build.py and rays/build.py generate -- the
# tables, the drawers and compiled tiles, the maps' bytes, the assembled demos
# -- is rebuilt from shared/ and the Python models.
examples/isoblocks/painter/output/
examples/isoblocks/rays/output/

# fast3d: the tables and the assembled checks and demo, from build.py.
examples/fast3d/output/

# Vendored reference copy of github.com/simon-frankau/head-over-heels -- a
# reverse-engineered disassembly of Head Over Heels, kept to hand while its
# Filmation engine design is ported into examples/filmation. Never committed:
# it carries the game's own bytes (memimage/memimage.z80, tape/*.tzx,
# bin/*.bin sprites), it is someone else's project tree rather than ours, and
# it states no licence at all. Same treatment as .chips-codegen-src/ and the
# game snapshots above. Clone it yourself:
#   git clone https://github.com/simon-frankau/head-over-heels vendor/head-over-heels
vendor/head-over-heels/

# What scripts/package_release.py stages into a copy of the extension for a
# release -- never into the extension itself, but kept out in case.
vscode-extension/bin/
vscode-extension/roms/

# The web emulator, as web/build.py writes it.
web/site/
